0, 15, 15, 18295, 2600, 283, 220, 20, 15, 15, 68, 24, 5639, 2600, 13, 1368, 220, 16, 4763, 50252, 11, 4779, 11222, 220, 20, 15, 15, 68, 24, 26259, 2600, 11, 864, 369, 777, 1056, 220, 17, 15, 68, 24, 11, 748, 12204, 6608, 13, 14235, 47675, 1332, 4779, 13001, 13, 10380, 220, 16, 15, 5639, 50252, 25, 220, 20, 15, 15, 68, 24, 14, 16, 15, 283, 220, 20, 15, 68, 24, 26259, 2600, 11, 1990, 835, 17, 15, 13, 14235, 4570, 12204, 466, 4918, 5639, 13, 6558, 579, 1236, 21966, 220, 16, 15, 15, 17567, 50, 30, 17722, 21966, 6970, 220, 17, 15, 28192, 1537, 4851, 30, 14235, 4145, 13, 79762, 220, 16, 15, 15, 11, 15, 15, 15, 7896, 506, 220, 17, 49580, 283, 220, 17, 15, 15, 17567, 50, 13, 13453, 220, 20, 15], "content": "We need answer user's request: explain to curious high-school student, about 400 words, why GPU limited by memory bandwidth rather than compute. Use one concrete analogy, simple worked example with made-up plausible numbers, finish explaining what engineers do. Need final only, no preamble. Need about 400 words. Let's draft around 400 words. Need ensure high-school accessible.\n\nNeed maybe count words. Let's craft ~400.\n\nDraft:\nImagine a graphics card as a kitchen with 100 chefs (compute units) and a narrow hallway to the pantry (memory). The chefs can chop, mix, and fry very fast, but if the hallway only lets in a few ingredients per second, the chefs spend most of their time waiting. That waiting is memory bandwidth: the rate at which data can move between the GPU\u2019s fast on-chip memory and the slower main memory.\n\nA GPU often needs to read lots of data: textures, vertex positions, colors, depth values, and intermediate results. If the data stream is too slow, the arithmetic units sit idle even though they could do more calculations. This is called being memory-bandwidth-bound.\n\nExample: suppose a GPU has 10,000 arithmetic units, each doing 1 operation per clock, and it runs at 2 GHz. That is 20,000,000,000 operations per second, or 20 GOPS. Now suppose its memory bandwidth is 500 GB/s. If each operation needs to read 1 byte from memory, the memory can supply only 500,000,000,000 bytes per second, so it could support 500 billion operations if one byte per op? Wait 500 GB/s = 500e9 bytes/s. If 1 byte/op, memory supports 500e9 ops/s, which is more than 20e9, so compute bound. Need plausible where memory limits. Maybe 10 bytes/op: 500e9/10 = 50e9 ops/s, still >20. Need lower compute or higher bytes. Let's make GPU 100 GOPS? Modern GPU maybe 20 TFLOPS? Need simple. Suppose 100,000 units at 2 GHz = 200 GOPS. Memory 50", "top2": [[[1596, -0.0025676642544567585], [22916, -6.587316989898682]], [[1144, -9.536788638797589e-06], [513, -12.632795333862305]], [[4087, -0.6349884271621704], [310, -0.7911831140518188]], [[1156, -0.003743553999811411], [310, -5.760604381561279]], [[579, -0.18702742457389832], [25, -1.9067195653915405]], [[1622, -0.00014616127009503543], [3296, -9.356926918029785]], [[25, -0.4228259325027466], [13, -1.1506587266921997]], [[10033, -0.33072635531425476], [79091, -1.6851249933242798]], [[310, -0.01302800327539444], [303, -4.361214637756348]], [[21520, -0.21333888173103333], [1496, -1.6631680727005005]], [[1496, -0.00938430055975914], [32308, -4.678249359130859]], [[33541, -0.02950829081237316], [2812, -3.5384461879730225]], [[5286, -0.00027547101490199566], [261, -8.3285551071167]], [[11, -0.4349576532840729], [303, -1.3151837587356567]], [[883, -0.08009933680295944], [3835, -3.244924783706665]], [[220, -4.172333774477011e-06], [19, -12.84250545501709]], [[19, -2.0265784769435413e-05], [20, -11.335299491882324]], [[15, -1.2755474926962052e-05], [18, -12.164052963256836]], [[15, -8.344653679159819e-07], [16, -15.045870780944824]], [[4105, -5.245222382654902e-06], [3069, -12.737142562866211]], [[11, -0.0041310167871415615], [13, -5.523892879486084]], [[3069, -1.5139694369281642e-05], [33488, -12.57813835144043]], [[21966, -0.5855614542961121], [13775, -0.8702390193939209]], [[6973, -0.5029644966125488], [62